1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:05,359
October twenty-ninth, 2018.

2
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:09,980
Lion Air Flight 610 departed
Jakarta, Indonesia.

3
00:00:10,616 --> 00:00:14,602
Thirteen minutes later, the
aircraft crashed into the Java Sea.

4
00:00:15,038 --> 00:00:16,981
One hundred and eighty-nine people died.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:23,095
Less than five months later, Ethiopian
Airlines Flight 302 crashed shortly after

6
00:00:23,135 --> 00:00:24,665
departing Addis Ababa.

7
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:27,906
Another one hundred and
fifty-seven people died.

8
00:00:28,408 --> 00:00:33,157
Two aircraft. Two countries. Three
hundred and forty-six lives lost.

9
00:00:33,645 --> 00:00:37,136
Both aircraft were Boeing 737 MAX jets.

10
00:00:37,692 --> 00:00:41,295
And the investigations would reveal
a deeply troubling connection.

11
00:00:43,198 --> 00:00:47,786
Following the second crash, aviation
authorities around the world began grounding

12
00:00:47,826 --> 00:00:49,556
the 737 MAX.

13
00:00:50,118 --> 00:00:56,054
On March thirteenth, 2019, the United States
Federal Aviation Administration ordered

14
00:00:56,094 --> 00:00:57,604
the aircraft grounded.

15
00:00:58,091 --> 00:01:00,029
Airlines canceled flights.

16
00:01:00,909 --> 00:01:02,361
Deliveries were disrupted.

17
00:01:02,948 --> 00:01:08,495
And one of the world's largest aerospace
manufacturers faced a crisis that went far

18
00:01:08,535 --> 00:01:10,365
beyond financial losses.

19
00:01:10,912 --> 00:01:16,186
How had a modern commercial aircraft,
developed by an experienced manufacturer and

20
00:01:16,226 --> 00:01:21,416
certified for passenger service, become
the subject of two catastrophic accidents?

21
00:01:21,974 --> 00:01:25,360
The answer begins years
earlier, with a competitor.

22
00:01:27,210 --> 00:01:31,534
In 2010, Airbus launched
the A320neo program.

23
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,357
The promise was straightforward.

24
00:01:34,114 --> 00:01:39,220
New engines would improve fuel efficiency,
giving airlines a compelling reason to

25
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:40,410
upgrade their fleets.

26
00:01:40,997 --> 00:01:44,565
For Boeing, this presented a
serious competitive challenge.

27
00:01:45,087 --> 00:01:50,510
Its 737 was one of the most successful
commercial aircraft families in history.

28
00:01:50,982 --> 00:01:54,259
But airlines increasingly
wanted better fuel economics.

29
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:56,702
Boeing needed an answer.

30
00:01:57,141 --> 00:02:01,978
And the decision it made would shape
the development of the 737 MAX.

31
00:02:03,783 --> 00:02:07,012
Boeing could pursue an
entirely new aircraft design.

32
00:02:07,557 --> 00:02:11,223
Or it could update the
existing 737 platform.

33
00:02:11,785 --> 00:02:13,645
It chose the second approach.

34
00:02:14,204 --> 00:02:18,428
In 2011, Boeing launched
the 737 MAX program.

35
00:02:19,034 --> 00:02:22,114
The strategy offered important
commercial advantages.

36
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:27,736
An established aircraft family, an existing
customer base, and the prospect of

37
00:02:27,776 --> 00:02:31,406
limiting the differences pilots
and airlines would have to manage.

38
00:02:31,906 --> 00:02:37,066
But updating a decades-old design with
larger, more efficient engines created

39
00:02:37,106 --> 00:02:39,136
engineering challenges.

40
00:02:39,832 --> 00:02:43,173
And one of the solutions would become
central to the investigations.

41
00:02:45,122 --> 00:02:50,901
The 737 MAX used larger engines positioned
differently from those on earlier 737

42
00:02:50,941 --> 00:02:51,941
models.

43
00:02:52,528 --> 00:02:57,147
These changes affected the aircraft's
aerodynamic behavior in certain flight

44
00:02:57,187 --> 00:02:58,187
conditions.

45
00:02:58,506 --> 00:03:02,489
Boeing developed a flight-control function
called the Maneuvering Characteristics

46
00:03:02,529 --> 00:03:05,279
Augmentation System.

47
00:03:05,709 --> 00:03:10,419
MCAS was intended to help produce handling
characteristics that met certification

48
00:03:10,459 --> 00:03:11,569
requirements.

49
00:03:12,178 --> 00:03:17,387
A flight-control system isn't inherently
dangerous simply because it's automated.

50
00:03:17,996 --> 00:03:23,013
The critical questions are how it receives
information, what authority it has, and

51
00:03:23,053 --> 00:03:25,103
what happens when something goes wrong.

52
00:03:26,776 --> 00:03:31,560
MCAS could automatically command
nose-down stabilizer trim under specific

53
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,050
manual-flight conditions.

54
00:03:33,795 --> 00:03:38,034
In normal circumstances, the system was
intended to operate without demanding

55
00:03:38,074 --> 00:03:40,044
special attention from pilots.

56
00:03:40,783 --> 00:03:44,866
But its original design created
a dangerous possibility.

57
00:03:45,513 --> 00:03:50,126
If the system received incorrect information
indicating an excessive angle of

58
00:03:50,166 --> 00:03:55,456
attack, it could command nose-down trim
when that correction was not needed.

59
00:03:55,901 --> 00:04:00,807
And the consequences depended on how the
aircraft, the automation, and the flight

60
00:04:00,847 --> 00:04:02,077
crew interacted.

61
00:04:02,589 --> 00:04:05,640
The next design choice made
that problem more serious.

62
00:04:07,577 --> 00:04:12,558
The original MCAS design relied on information
from a single angle-of-attack sensor

63
00:04:12,598 --> 00:04:13,948
at a time.

64
00:04:14,353 --> 00:04:19,069
That sensor helps indicate the angle between
the aircraft's reference line and the

65
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:20,119
approaching airflow.

66
00:04:20,754 --> 00:04:25,492
An erroneous reading could therefore
trigger MCAS even when the aircraft was not

67
00:04:25,532 --> 00:04:27,074
approaching a stall.

68
00:04:27,114 --> 00:04:32,204
The system could also activate repeatedly
after pilot counter-trim inputs.

69
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:37,778
Investigators later identified serious
weaknesses in the assumptions used to assess

70
00:04:37,818 --> 00:04:38,818
these hazards.

71
00:04:39,412 --> 00:04:44,103
A single faulty input had become capable
of creating a much larger flight-control

72
00:04:44,143 --> 00:04:45,333
problem.

73
00:04:46,976 --> 00:04:48,963
There was another critical issue.

74
00:04:49,364 --> 00:04:54,908
Many 737 MAX pilots had not been informed
about MCAS before the first accident.

75
00:04:55,396 --> 00:04:59,371
Boeing's safety assessments relied partly
on assumptions about how quickly crews

76
00:04:59,411 --> 00:05:04,141
would recognize and respond to an
unexpected stabilizer-trim problem.

77
00:05:04,556 --> 00:05:07,805
But real emergencies are
rarely clean or simple.

78
00:05:08,274 --> 00:05:09,915
Warnings can arrive together.

79
00:05:10,458 --> 00:05:12,198
Workload can escalate.

80
00:05:12,648 --> 00:05:16,372
And pilots may have seconds to
understand conflicting information.

81
00:05:17,093 --> 00:05:21,632
Investigators questioned whether the
system's design and certification adequately

82
00:05:21,672 --> 00:05:24,042
accounted for those human realities.

83
00:05:25,924 --> 00:05:32,217
On October twenty-ninth, 2018, Lion
Air Flight 610 experienced erroneous

84
00:05:32,257 --> 00:05:33,467
angle-of-attack information.

85
00:05:34,191 --> 00:05:38,693
The resulting system behavior included
repeated nose-down trim commands associated

86
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:41,183
with MCAS.

87
00:05:41,739 --> 00:05:46,423
The crew struggled with multiple indications
and flight-control difficulties.

88
00:05:47,043 --> 00:05:51,775
The aircraft crashed into the Java
Sea, killing everyone on board.

89
00:05:52,218 --> 00:05:57,084
Indonesia's investigation identified a
combination of contributing factors,

90
00:05:57,194 --> 00:06:02,274
including design assumptions, system
behavior, maintenance issues, and the crew's

91
00:06:02,314 --> 00:06:03,314
circumstances.

92
00:06:03,732 --> 00:06:07,509
The accident exposed weaknesses
that demanded urgent attention.

93
00:06:08,136 --> 00:06:11,357
But the 737 MAX remained
in commercial service.

94
00:06:13,141 --> 00:06:19,806
On March tenth, 2019, Ethiopian Airlines
Flight 302 departed Addis Ababa for

95
00:06:19,846 --> 00:06:20,846
Nairobi.

96
00:06:21,396 --> 00:06:25,909
Soon after takeoff, erroneous angle-of-attack
information again contributed to

97
00:06:25,949 --> 00:06:28,239
unwanted MCAS activation.

98
00:06:28,943 --> 00:06:32,524
The crew faced a rapidly
deteriorating situation.

99
00:06:32,969 --> 00:06:37,449
The aircraft crashed, killing all one
hundred and fifty-seven people aboard.

100
00:06:37,987 --> 00:06:40,316
The two accidents were not identical.

101
00:06:40,921 --> 00:06:45,908
Investigators have disagreed about some
technical details of the Ethiopian accident.

102
00:06:46,449 --> 00:06:52,108
But the central role of MCAS and related
system vulnerabilities became impossible to

103
00:06:52,148 --> 00:06:53,148
ignore.

104
00:06:53,193 --> 00:06:54,854
The crisis was now global.

105
00:06:56,636 --> 00:07:01,283
As investigators examined Boeing's internal
records and the aircraft's certification

106
00:07:01,323 --> 00:07:04,753
process, the story became more disturbing.

107
00:07:05,249 --> 00:07:10,215
A United States congressional investigation
identified production pressure, flawed

108
00:07:10,255 --> 00:07:15,455
technical assumptions, failures to communicate
crucial information, and inadequate

109
00:07:15,495 --> 00:07:17,165
regulatory oversight.

110
00:07:17,752 --> 00:07:21,967
The report described how commercial goals
influenced the program's development.

111
00:07:22,475 --> 00:07:27,676
It also examined information that had not
been adequately shared with regulators and

112
00:07:27,716 --> 00:07:28,716
pilots.

113
00:07:28,884 --> 00:07:32,727
This was no longer simply a question
of whether one software function had

114
00:07:32,767 --> 00:07:33,837
malfunctioned.

115
00:07:34,106 --> 00:07:37,067
It was a question of how the
system had been approved.

116
00:07:38,871 --> 00:07:42,895
Commercial aviation relies on
multiple layers of protection.

117
00:07:43,372 --> 00:07:45,441
Engineers identify hazards.

118
00:07:46,076 --> 00:07:48,297
Manufacturers assess risks.

119
00:07:48,893 --> 00:07:50,414
Regulators review compliance.

120
00:07:51,189 --> 00:07:56,061
Pilots receive the information and training
needed to operate aircraft safely.

121
00:07:56,610 --> 00:08:02,108
In the 737 MAX program, investigators
found weaknesses across several of those

122
00:08:02,148 --> 00:08:03,148
layers.

123
00:08:03,273 --> 00:08:08,566
The Federal Aviation Administration's
technical review identified problems in safety

124
00:08:08,606 --> 00:08:12,936
assessments, including how changes
to MCAS had been evaluated.

125
00:08:13,559 --> 00:08:17,577
Congressional investigators also
questioned the effectiveness of delegated

126
00:08:17,617 --> 00:08:19,227
certification oversight.

127
00:08:19,832 --> 00:08:23,056
The deeper problem was
not one failed safeguard.

128
00:08:23,482 --> 00:08:25,586
It was the interaction of several.

129
00:08:27,387 --> 00:08:30,858
The grounding carried enormous
financial consequences.

130
00:08:31,407 --> 00:08:36,845
In July 2019, Boeing announced an after-tax
charge of approximately four point nine

131
00:08:36,885 --> 00:08:42,935
billion dollars related to estimated customer
compensation and other considerations.

132
00:08:43,729 --> 00:08:49,054
The disruption also affected production,
deliveries, airline schedules, and Boeing's

133
00:08:49,094 --> 00:08:50,094
earnings.

134
00:08:50,174 --> 00:08:54,560
In 2019, its commercial airplanes
division reported an operating loss of

135
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:57,770
approximately six point
seven billion dollars.

136
00:08:58,607 --> 00:09:01,950
But those figures are not a
single total cost of the crisis.

137
00:09:02,663 --> 00:09:06,673
And no financial calculation can adequately
represent the loss of three hundred and

138
00:09:06,713 --> 00:09:08,803
forty-six lives.

139
00:09:10,652 --> 00:09:15,799
Boeing and aviation regulators worked on
changes to the aircraft and its operating

140
00:09:15,839 --> 00:09:16,869
requirements.

141
00:09:17,425 --> 00:09:22,644
The revised MCAS design introduced
additional safeguards, including comparing

142
00:09:22,684 --> 00:09:27,674
information from both angle-of-attack
sensors and limiting the system's authority.

143
00:09:28,166 --> 00:09:32,072
Pilot training and operational
procedures were also revised.

144
00:09:32,802 --> 00:09:40,482
In November 2020, the FAA authorized the
737 MAX to return to commercial service in

145
00:09:40,522 --> 00:09:44,812
the United States, subject to
required modifications and training.

146
00:09:45,393 --> 00:09:49,549
The aircraft returned to service in
stages across different jurisdictions.

147
00:09:50,348 --> 00:09:54,597
But restoring certification was
not the same as restoring trust.

148
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:01,211
So was the 737 MAX crisis caused
by competition with Airbus?

149
00:10:01,941 --> 00:10:05,543
Competition was an important
part of the commercial context.

150
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:09,149
But competition alone did
not cause the crashes.

151
00:10:09,798 --> 00:10:12,517
The evidence points to
a more complex failure.

152
00:10:13,014 --> 00:10:14,619
Design decisions.

153
00:10:15,148 --> 00:10:17,051
Incorrect safety assumptions.

154
00:10:17,794 --> 00:10:19,239
Insufficient communication.

155
00:10:20,005 --> 00:10:22,330
Weaknesses in certification oversight.

156
00:10:22,979 --> 00:10:28,129
And a failure to adequately anticipate how
pilots would respond to the system under

157
00:10:28,169 --> 00:10:29,499
real-world conditions.

158
00:10:30,234 --> 00:10:35,598
The catastrophe emerged when multiple
defenses failed to prevent a dangerous design

159
00:10:35,638 --> 00:10:38,108
from entering and remaining in service.

160
00:10:39,956 --> 00:10:45,575
The Boeing 737 MAX crisis is a warning about
the distance between commercial success

161
00:10:45,615 --> 00:10:47,765
and engineering responsibility.

162
00:10:48,705 --> 00:10:54,248
An aircraft program can meet deadlines,
win customers, and appear competitive.

163
00:10:54,888 --> 00:10:59,370
But none of those achievements can
substitute for rigorous safety design and

164
00:10:59,410 --> 00:11:00,640
independent oversight.

165
00:11:01,342 --> 00:11:05,773
The critical question is not whether a
company can move faster than its rivals.

166
00:11:06,425 --> 00:11:10,805
It's whether its decisions remain
safe when assumptions prove wrong.

167
00:11:11,289 --> 00:11:13,837
For Boeing, the consequences
were devastating.

168
00:11:14,632 --> 00:11:18,029
Three hundred and forty-six
people lost their lives.

169
00:11:18,562 --> 00:11:22,930
And the evidence exposed failures
far beyond one piece of software.

170
00:11:23,297 --> 00:11:25,260
Every brand leaves evidence.

